## 3. Implementation spec

### 3.1 Global palette and fog helper
Add one `FOG` config near the top of `RENDER`:
```js
const FOG = {
  sky: ["#EFEFEF", "#FBFBFB", "#FFFFFF"],      // top → mid → horizon
  layers: ["#E2E2E2", "#CDCDCD", "#B2B2B2", "#8E8E8E", "#5E5E5E"], // far → near
  mist: "rgba(255,255,255,0.55)",              // fog band wash
  ground: "#000000",
  rim: "rgba(255,255,255,0.22)"                // light rim on black silhouettes
};
```
Each level may tint the grays very slightly to keep its identity (for example, Frostbite Ridge cool `#E6EAEE`, Ashfall Caldera warm `#E8E2DE`, Sunken Reef blue-gray). Add an optional 7th entry to each `th` array, or a lookup keyed by level `n` inside `RENDER`, **not** in `LEVELS` gameplay fields. Saturation must stay below about 8% so hazard colors still dominate.

### 3.2 Sky
Replace the flat white clear with a vertical linear gradient using `FOG.sky`. Add a wide, soft radial "horizon glow" ellipse centered around 60% of the screen height. Cache the gradient and rebuild it only in `resize()`.

### 3.3 Backdrop layers (biggest change)
Rewrite `BACKDROPS.strip()` so it draws **solid filled silhouettes** instead of screentone:
- Remove the hatching `pattern()` fill and the black outlines. `F()` fills with the layer's flat gray, `K()` uses a slightly darker shade of the same layer, and `Wt()` uses a slightly lighter shade (windows and highlights become subtle tone shifts, not white).
- Change `strip()` to take a `tone` (fill color) argument and an optional `softness` value. For far layers, draw at about half resolution and upscale with `imageSmoothingEnabled = true` so they look naturally blurred. A one-time `ctx.filter = "blur(2px)"` on the **offscreen** canvas is also fine, with a feature check for Safari.
- Bake a **vertical fog gradient** into the bottom 35% of each strip (from transparent to `FOG.mist`) so each layer's base fades into mist.
- Build **5 strips** instead of 2 in `buildStrips()`:
  | # | Content | Scale | Parallax factor | Tone |
  |---|---|---|---|---|
  | 0 | soft rolling hills + distant spire/landmark (`th[0]`) | `.4` | `.05` | `layers[0]` |
  | 1 | far version of `th[0]` (forest canopies / theme landmark) | `.5` | `.1` | `layers[1]` |
  | 2 | mid `th[1]` | `.62` | `.18` | `layers[2]` |
  | 3 | near `th[1]` (larger, sparser) | `.8` | `.26` | `layers[3]` |
  | 4 | big foreground-ish canopy trees at the screen edges, partly cut off | `1.0` | `.34` | `layers[4]` |
  Keep the existing seamless wrap logic (the 3× draw with `-off`). Bump `stripKey` so the cache invalidates.
- Between strips, draw a full-width **horizontal mist band** (a cached gradient rect) every frame. This is cheap and gives the layered fog look.
- **New generator: `canopy`.** Add a broad tree generator to `G`: a slightly curved trunk with 2–4 branches, and a canopy made of 8–14 overlapping circles/ellipses clustered at branch tips (lumpy, cloud-like outline), plus 3–6 thin hanging moss strands. Use it for layer 4 on organic levels (Whispering Pines, Paper Kite Plains, Bamboo Monastery, Hanging Gardens, Sunken Reef (as kelp-like), Comet Steppe, Mirage Dunes (as palms)). For urban/industrial levels, layer 4 uses large versions of the level's `th[1]` generator.
- **Restyle the existing generators** (`peaks`, `pines`, `towers`, `city`, `gears`, `ferris`, `columns`, and so on) to suit solid silhouette fills: drop line-only details thinner than about `.05` units on far layers, and round off sharp corners where it reads as organic.

### 3.4 Ambient particles
Keep the `AMB` types and motion. Draw particles in the gray of the depth they "belong" to (map `p.z` to `layers[1..3]`). Add an extra low-alpha drifting **fog wisp** type (large soft ellipses, alpha ≈ .08–.15) that is always present, with 4–6 instances moving slowly left. Skip wisps when `quality < 1`.

### 3.5 Terrain (foreground)
In the `/* terrain */` loop:
- **Remove the white `strokeRect` outline.** Terrain becomes pure `FOG.ground`.
- **Rough cliff faces:** on the left and right vertical edges of each segment, where the neighboring segment is lower or there is a gap, draw a jagged edge: a polyline that juts in and out by `0–0.12` units every `0.25` units, seeded with `hsh(x)` so it is stable. It must stay **inside** the collision box (only notch inward, never extend outward past `xa`/`xb`).
- **Top edge foliage:** along `Y(q.top)`, draw silhouetted grass tufts (clusters of 3–7 thin curved blades, `0.15–0.45` units tall), occasional ferns or broad leaves, and small sprouts. Density and style depend on the theme's `th[2]`:
  - `grass`, `sand`, `snow`, `tiles`, `bricks` → tufts (snow: smaller, rounder clumps; sand: sparse dry grass)
  - `roof`, `rivets`, `girder`, `train`, `hull`, `planks` → no grass; small bolts, vents, coils of rope, chimney stubs
  - `rock`, `obsidian` → pebbles, small crystal stubs
  - `checker`, `books` → candle stubs, scattered pages
  Foliage is purely decorative and drawn **above** the top edge, so keep it under 0.45 units and sparse right where the runner lands. Skip it within ±0.6 units of any spike or hazard x-position so silhouettes don't merge.
- **Light rim:** draw a 1–1.5px `FOG.rim` line along the very top edge and the cliff faces so black terrain separates from dark backdrop layer 4.
- **Re-skin `groundDetail()`:** the current white interior lines become **very dark gray** (`#141414`–`#1C1C1C`) so the body reads as solid black silhouette with only faint texture, as in the reference.
- **Flipped (Zenith) terrain:** apply the same treatment mirrored, with foliage hanging downward like roots/moss. Keep the colored `COL.Z` edge line exactly as is.

### 3.6 Props
Rewrite `prop()` as silhouettes in the spirit of the reference: **wooden signposts** (post + 1–2 angled boards), bushes, ferns, small rocks, mushrooms, lanterns and crates, picked per theme. Keep the existing placement logic in `setLevel()` (quiet chart cells).

### 3.7 Static obstacles (`case "L"` only: spikes, posts, rocks, bars, stalactites, knives)
These are lethal but **not** interactive hazards. Restyle the art only and keep the exact footprint:
- Replace `ink()`'s white `0.13` keyline with: solid black fill, plus a thin `FOG.rim` stroke (≈ `.035` units) along the **upper** edges only, giving the soft light edge visible on the spikes in the reference.
- Default triangle spikes (`tri`): crisp, slightly concave sides, sharp tips, the same `hw`/`hh`. Keep the per-style variants (`thorn`, `shard`, `cactus`, `bone`, `coral`, `flame`, `cone`, `mast`) but render them as silhouettes with the rim.
- **Hanging bars (`bar()`):** when a bar hangs from the ceiling (the `up = 16` case), draw it as in the reference: a black slab with grass tufts on top, hung from **two chains** (alternating oval links, about `.12` units, black with rim), and, for `pillar`/`beam`/`lintel` styles, a row of downward triangle teeth along the underside **only if those teeth fall inside the existing bar hitbox**. Otherwise make them decorative bevels that stay within the box.
- The spinning knife (`o.v === "kn"`): black silhouette with rim; keep the rotation.

### 3.8 Player (`stick()`)
- Remove the white keyline. The stickman becomes a solid black silhouette with a slightly larger round head (about +10%) and thicker limbs (about +15%) for readability against dark layers, plus a 1px `FOG.rim` outline on the head only.
- **Square pixel trail:** while running and airborne, emit small black squares (0.08–0.16 units) from the hips every ~45 ms. They drift slightly up and back, shrink and fade over about 0.35 s, as in the reference image. Add this as a new `fx` kind (`k: 3`, square, using the existing `fx` loop) so it reuses the pool. Cap it at about 24 live squares, and halve that when `quality < 1`.
- Keep all animation timing, IK, hang and flip transforms unchanged.

### 3.9 Death, jump and landing FX
Restyle `burst()` and `emit()` particles to black squares and shards (silhouette style). Keep any FX already colored by a hazard (rings using `COL[...]`) unchanged.

### 3.10 Finish gate
Keep the position and size. Draw the poles as silhouette posts with rim; keep the checker banner but slightly soften the white squares to `#EDEDED`.

### 3.11 HUD (play screen)
Restyle the in-game HUD to match the reference **without changing what data it shows or the element IDs that `game.js` writes to**:
- **Pause button:** rounded square (radius ≈ 10px), 3px black outline, white fill, two black rounded bars.
- **Progress bar:** long pill across the top center (≈ 55% of the screen width), 3px black outline, white track, solid black fill, with 3px inner padding so the fill has a white gap from the outline.
- **Percent readout:** right of the bar, heavy rounded type (`font-weight: 900`, system rounded stack), white fill with a 2–3px black text stroke (`-webkit-text-stroke` plus a `paint-order` fallback).
- **Attempt counter:** "Attempt N" at top-right, same type treatment, slightly smaller. If no attempt label exists yet, bind it to the existing try counter (`G.tryN`). This is display-only.
- Keep safe-area insets (`--top`, `--pad`) working on notched phones.

### 3.12 Home screen and level select
The home demo uses `RENDER` with `th: null` / the home theme, so make sure the new fog layers render there too (use the Whispering Pines palette as the default). Level cards and chips may stay as they are; optionally tint their backgrounds with each level's fog palette.

